"Graduated Driver License (GDL) and Hardship License | Department of Public Safety (https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/graduated-driver-license-gdl-program-summary)\nciteturn31view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/graduated-driver-license-gdl-program-summary\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/graduated-driver-license-gdl-and-hardship-license; Total lines: 53\nL0: Search Form Search\nL1: \nL2: [Input: What can we help you find?]\nL3: \nL4: [Button: Search]\nL5: \nL6: [Input] [Input]\nL7: \nL8: ## Change Text Size\nL9: \nL10: ^{-}A A ^{+}A\nL11: \nL12: ## Change Display Setting\nL13: \nL14: cite0†Default cite1†Text Only L15: \nL16: # Graduated Driver License (GDL) and Hardship License\nL17: \nL18: September 28, 2020\nL19: \nL20: The Graduated Driver License (GDL) program had two phases designed to provide young drivers with a safe and structured learning environment before granting full driving privileges.\nL21: #### cite2†Phase I: Learner License L22:   * Applicants under 18 must hold a valid learners license or minor restricted driver license (MRDL) for at least six months to be eligible for a provisional license.\nL23:   * Learner License holders must be supervised by a licensed adult when operating a motor vehicle.\nL24:   * Out-of-state transfers are required to obtain a Texas learner license and complete the six-month holding period, regardless of prior licensure or wait until 18 to obtain a driver license without the six-month holding period.\nL25:   * License suspensions or revocations during this phase will extend the holding period by the suspension or revocation duration.\nL26: #### cite3†Phase II: Provisional License L27:   * Eligibility for a provisional license requires:\nL28:     * Holding a valid learner license or MRDL for at least six months.\nL29:     * Reaching the age of 16.\nL30:     * Completing a state-approved driver education course (classroom and behind-the-wheel)\nL31:     * Completing the Impact Texas Teen Driver (ITTD) program\nL32:   * Provisional license holders under 18 years are subject to the following restrictions:\nL33:     * No more than one passenger under 21 (unless family) is permitted in the vehicle.\nL34:     * Driving is prohibited between midnight and 5:00 am except for work, school, or emergencies.\nL35:     * Use of any wireless communication devices while driving is strictly forbidden (including hands-free)\nL36: Additional Considerations\nL37: \nL38:   * The GDL program does not apply to motorcycle licenses.\nL39:   * Out-of-state transfers may have different requirements during the application process.\nL40: \nL41: By adhering to the GDL program's structure and practicing safe driving habits, young drivers in Texas can progressively gain experience and ultimately obtain a full driver license.\nL42: \nL43: Hardship License\nL44: A minor's restricted driver license (MRDL), also known as a hardship license, may be issued under certain instances where it has been established that there is a necessity to drive and the applicant is under 16 years of age. A hardship license applicant must complete all of the regular licensing requirements listed above, with the exception of holding a learner license for a minimum of six months, and must be at least 15 years of age. A hardship license expires on the applicant's next birthday.\nL45: For more information and to review the types of hardships that qualify for a hardship license, please review the cite4†hardship license application .\nL46: ### Follow DPS\nL47: \nL48: ### Keep Texas Safe\nL49: \nL50: cite5†Report Suspicious Activity†iwatchtx.org L51: \nL52: © 2026 Texas Department of Public Safety. PDF files require cite6†Adobe Reader†get.adobe.com or compatible."
